#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134760} :activity/id #uuid "e1b56295-70ee-55b2-8a65-97f8ae733716" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135084 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nA similar result, known as **the fundamental theorem for line integrals,** exists for line integrals of conservative vector fields. This theorem can be stated as follows:\n\n*Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ Then,*\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll discuss how this theorem is proved in simple cases at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's take a look at a concrete example of how to apply this theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "0c3e4762-a85a-5881-81ad-857e0bec5a48" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4306 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e063a5e6-8819-5814-8c62-dae890a57d71" :step/math-academy-id 26178 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133694 :question/id #uuid "234800a9-ae9c-56c4-8284-84041f129a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-8759"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538149} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538145}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a10e6c95-833b-528d-a1f9-411415a6a652" :step/math-academy-id 32622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134850}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133695 :question/id #uuid "667923e0-14bc-5bbd-8129-333703cd6616" :question/math-academy-id "e-8760"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530780} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530769}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "f869fbb2-2435-5488-b530-01a592efdf62" :step/math-academy-id 32627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133706 :question/id #uuid "3f724b05-571d-529d-ab2b-db9dd6d2b531" :question/math-academy-id "e-9502"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049633}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259594}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"} :step/id #uuid "879e7253-5b1c-559d-8414-14057286be07" :step/math-academy-id 35588 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135127 :tutorial/content "Let's apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y) = x^2y^3$ and $C$ is the path along the curve $y=x^2$ for $x \\in [0,2].$ \n\nFirst, we must parameterize the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t^2\\right\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of $C$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(0)\t= \\left\\langle 0, 0\\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{r}(2)\t= \\left\\langle 2, 4\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the path's endpoints defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,4)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3}-{0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice how much faster it is to evaluate a line integral when we can use the fundamental theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "8ce39e74-c98d-54af-8d93-20f6b5bca39a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5829 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "15f14408-48b8-5f0f-8c55-d5009dc56e57" :step/math-academy-id 35721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135128 :tutorial/content "Here, we prove the fundamental theorem of line integrals in the two-dimensional case. The three-dimensional case is similar.\n\nLet $C$ be a smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ We wish to show that\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nUsing our usual formula for calculating line integrals, we can write the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}∇f\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the integrand above is simply the derivative of $f(\\mathbf r(t))$ with respect to $t.$ Thus, we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(f(\\mathbf r(t)))\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the fundamental theorem of calculus, this is equal to \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nThis concludes the proof in the case where $C$ is smooth. It's easy to show that the theorem also holds for piecewise smooth curves." :tutorial/id #uuid "0ef0926c-7a69-5fc8-91f0-7f40e3be9be9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5830 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Fundamental Theorem for Smooth Curves"} :step/id #uuid "48691aa9-a37a-52cd-a44c-e9ef2b10ea85" :step/math-academy-id 35722}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133996}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423278} :activity/id #uuid "f437e194-169c-50f2-945d-004eb6df919b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324fc058-3716-52e2-915c-a8ff8548ce66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238492} {:answer/id #uuid "40f10544-0cd1-50c6-9897-c035b59965c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238616} {:answer/id #uuid "99009bfa-183e-50ad-b31d-4a8cd8b1b4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239384} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9e232f-a132-5ae1-a985-ee43b1e859ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239575} {:answer/id #uuid "b4578f1a-d647-5132-b9c6-b097afd1fc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc00398-8a1c-5911-a2b1-71601fb149ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45e3e133-a99c-58ca-b74f-4cbfe235bb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-104625" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "47da3408-dc6c-5c1f-9665-3466da3da017" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c9589f-c628-5f4a-a78a-70eef556a95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69fec3-73fc-5815-8bc3-797953319185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} {:answer/id #uuid "8020ab34-3c45-5159-8e7f-6c5ce658702c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257922} {:answer/id #uuid "da2ca4d2-0f0c-5594-9b9e-6e91ba3983b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258687} {:answer/id #uuid "e253a7c1-631c-5b5e-ba82-916317b443b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c80ca6-5207-5a6c-a174-ad7e192a275e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388c57e6-e07f-5b15-a475-f9e96568702d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124028" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5dece436-1610-5dc1-95d0-9fdd97342268"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adb26c2-291f-5ecb-be27-a83497de5b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238279} {:answer/id #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} {:answer/id #uuid "add0b6a3-0052-52a8-a4b1-57278ac77df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239563} {:answer/id #uuid "b0815d5d-2eab-5eb6-b0b6-cea74e67c474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239588} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f6a-3c28-5f17-b90f-9614a603b361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f03b38-7ac7-5b2a-bebf-d97ba46b022c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b025c1d-a393-5ea5-8c4e-6e0786f6f25d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104617" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84d1b7ff-6c49-52fd-9e1e-1f9250c2fd44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302b8581-4a68-5c5c-8795-9744e9d4a2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257206} {:answer/id #uuid "953e0310-1f6a-5dcb-9428-9966108f8f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258094} {:answer/id #uuid "b72e2f4d-4041-5680-a701-550b3147bc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258378} {:answer/id #uuid "ca82cadd-75e5-598b-bacc-17ec432f7824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258541} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2dc29a-3749-5531-9860-c3324dac8a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "003b8481-988f-56eb-a4c6-1362503d86f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124002" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c3aba54e-fa79-516c-9cb2-e92b39bb4228" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eea1d61-c31a-52a0-9c80-b705eeabdf54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256925} {:answer/id #uuid "32a24c80-640c-5f96-9d30-3886433c358a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257225} {:answer/id #uuid "89fe846b-c77b-5114-acc8-ae328a43149b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257994} {:answer/id #uuid "d080e35c-13c8-5910-bdbc-1433606170a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} {:answer/id #uuid "de2288dd-737a-5b6e-99cf-5d7174075a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ff9e85-14c9-5103-9111-1d260075e876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c23e8787-68ef-58ab-bf57-8f769ed64353" :question/math-academy-id "q-124003" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d90c6fd6-ba8c-5bad-bd78-dc112ba8ab5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423277}}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423275}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432467} :activity/id #uuid "cffde6ff-a784-5afc-aeec-783de80a05df" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb86dc4-a7cd-5ca1-b598-954b892dcccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238168} {:answer/id #uuid "23431fc6-f04f-5360-af6d-30876ddc94bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238365} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcde37b-9a32-506b-bf91-9dcabb76716e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239270} {:answer/id #uuid "9b44e22a-7576-5afa-b3b8-4a77db65eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239409} {:answer/id #uuid "a90ce1ba-c1cf-56f7-b6bb-6a5a1b7fdca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238168} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae2dfe3-e747-5345-997b-435580cc53ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "893763e7-56bb-5bf7-9f2b-99426982714b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104626" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "25e18fe5-7b73-527a-b337-8cc79c075b2e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432468}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a528a36-e97e-5732-8fae-1697a81d8ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238153} {:answer/id #uuid "44bbd131-4ecd-540b-ad14-259cefa4dcf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238651} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab4b784-0b5f-5e1b-9fde-ec17a7e2873d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238701} {:answer/id #uuid "89f290a7-499e-5b23-8f65-ac96513f25dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239255} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbad1630-befd-5b0d-9389-d98b46343814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1eb7495c-4bb1-5f06-bc1d-e458481de613" :question/math-academy-id "q-104623" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f65033c-fa07-530c-83d8-834ede13f3cc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432466}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30453404-690a-5b85-850f-9aaa09a4efe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} {:answer/id #uuid "35cdd507-7f75-51ad-acf4-534f3b62ea96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238522} {:answer/id #uuid "58c144e5-2b4f-5201-b0bb-e144657253c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2-63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238819} {:answer/id #uuid "da6b93e1-2f2d-5dd2-9235-e2c75c2150c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239960} {:answer/id #uuid "e08284d3-5a39-5136-ad35-3aadf74bdc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a6c211e-df9c-57af-8d94-442d4251996d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7794dd99-ebef-5626-aacd-8b9fce4aa6b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104733" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b6eb744f-99c4-5225-be69-a6e6f69476c0"}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432465}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521648} :activity/id #uuid "355c92f9-f7a2-57ec-ba82-f968b129cdab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb8367f-a159-5457-99f5-50133f64eb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256893} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa182ef-4a0a-5db2-a08b-55a80052cb00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256930} {:answer/id #uuid "228efe5e-c3c4-564d-9dab-7a9a4edeb67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257068} {:answer/id #uuid "3b05dbc7-a00e-579d-9183-316aebb35c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257306} {:answer/id #uuid "4e044364-8237-5de2-8e2f-2b3e611819bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257452} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3f4676-5218-53c9-a375-46cf12254dde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "615b0135-e131-5b5f-9638-4296b1aa2a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124005" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1911f355-c319-5a82-939d-0d7b2b9be6b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12811091-da24-5f9e-a3cc-d521c187f782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415069} {:answer/id #uuid "48539c5b-fd63-583d-8da2-566a4dd6e0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415077} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d2cb1e-5c01-5454-b9f6-ddf29ce394b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415082} {:answer/id #uuid "b864bbf5-46ca-5a26-aa58-751cd0107a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415087} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415091} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab184202-13b3-5378-a512-5889441d45af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6e8a51b9-ad21-56cd-a267-db39e81294ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104624" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =(3+{4}^{2})-(-1+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3+16)-(-1) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b2c21ccd-d4ea-555a-8e29-430aebe2f7e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d21652b-8d10-5c45-b44f-db44c924244e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257022} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad034e3-435a-54af-8b44-ed2fc0bf168f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257573} {:answer/id #uuid "863b68a3-5559-5883-8119-c9324587def7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257962} {:answer/id #uuid "a621cce8-352a-549d-ba06-938de99fba53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258228} {:answer/id #uuid "edd3b1c2-37e9-5a98-86ec-a93068bfe7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257573} :answer-field/id #uuid "27cdb6ef-9816-548d-bfcd-7bdee6ce6dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "530f83ac-873a-5207-ba3d-e9438dabfd29" :question/math-academy-id "q-124027" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={e}^{x}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,2)$ and $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {e}^{3+1}-0\\cdot {e}^{2+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ce79cbc6-e7a2-5282-9005-4ff166707378"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415071} {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} {:answer/id #uuid "75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415080} {:answer/id #uuid "87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415081} {:answer/id #uuid "db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" :question/math-academy-id "q-104637" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f((r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(0,2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{0(2)}-0\\cdot (1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d88e62ca-aba2-5795-a208-8e8ba9cb91b6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00980506-979a-5a3c-bb6b-3b50f009bfac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415067} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeef040-2421-5b1c-b55a-b92ee77dadcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415070} {:answer/id #uuid "5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415078} {:answer/id #uuid "a2214f1f-3631-5be2-85e4-d5102a1c44c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415083} {:answer/id #uuid "d6de32bd-280a-5c5a-aa34-19f1b3faadf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415092}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415078} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d412271-dad8-50a6-a5b4-2d3051bb421a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "63f96647-0e17-5269-a429-2d3bdcfb1cff" :question/math-academy-id "q-104616" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{3}+1)-({0}^{3}+0) \\\\  & =(27+1)-0 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dd7e742b-c593-52c1-a0b4-39558c9a3b1e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521631}}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520750}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539118} :activity/id #uuid "efcd5bcf-6bd6-550a-a3e0-2a0c3d298d56" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adb26c2-291f-5ecb-be27-a83497de5b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238279} {:answer/id #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} {:answer/id #uuid "add0b6a3-0052-52a8-a4b1-57278ac77df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239563} {:answer/id #uuid "b0815d5d-2eab-5eb6-b0b6-cea74e67c474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239588} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f6a-3c28-5f17-b90f-9614a603b361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f03b38-7ac7-5b2a-bebf-d97ba46b022c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b025c1d-a393-5ea5-8c4e-6e0786f6f25d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104617" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "26a80e97-a6dc-5747-813c-d34eb97de4f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f31b92-c386-50b2-b977-d94225bcfbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238253} {:answer/id #uuid "465fbdf4-7176-5e2a-afb9-7055988a9594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238665} {:answer/id #uuid "54e54a33-5402-591a-ac33-570a9653db6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238789} {:answer/id #uuid "796fc17c-d543-5b01-83ae-f85bfdb65ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239102} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd66caa-a9be-51dd-a8aa-57947da3bed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238789} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed60cbe-d21d-5310-989e-903acd1a44f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7507d02e-e1da-50a2-9a9d-6236d6a7afae" :question/math-academy-id "q-104745" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "49fc4c7e-267c-5cf4-a795-e02e380cf712" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5570ad04-a911-54fe-882f-cae6a0eccb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16-{e}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257514} {:answer/id #uuid "69d52aa2-f5a3-518e-9e37-8487d0ca7564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257719} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdee35c-5f2c-5453-8ea0-524f7e2d5558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257879} {:answer/id #uuid "a641ae1f-fce3-54ae-b9d3-8c7c4dd0c256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+{e}^{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258233} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f247a0-2670-5ca0-8d3b-f9534636b266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257879} :answer-field/id #uuid "399493dc-69ea-5245-b7ef-1fc5d184a6d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33d927c3-e1a0-5a24-bd3b-65369e4fc02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124030" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\in [1,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d45199d9-a99e-54aa-9c88-4b37e9921203"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a528a36-e97e-5732-8fae-1697a81d8ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238153} {:answer/id #uuid "44bbd131-4ecd-540b-ad14-259cefa4dcf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238651} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab4b784-0b5f-5e1b-9fde-ec17a7e2873d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238701} {:answer/id #uuid "89f290a7-499e-5b23-8f65-ac96513f25dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239255} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbad1630-befd-5b0d-9389-d98b46343814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1eb7495c-4bb1-5f06-bc1d-e458481de613" :question/math-academy-id "q-104623" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dbf368de-00bb-5e7f-9238-fe5059ee047c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c13dff-cd6b-59c8-8afc-6e8f8eabf8f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324856} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0e1662-5531-5797-9a2d-180d1aefa39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325032} {:answer/id #uuid "b060de89-9a07-5c08-8a5a-902923743941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325474} {:answer/id #uuid "df7d018d-9bcc-56b5-9a57-3f93a49339b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325922} {:answer/id #uuid "fba1ed0a-e6ae-5b81-9e9c-40ba51cc6012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326186} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5eacfc-70b8-5d6e-b1f9-f5af4734a6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "73c892c9-a1e1-5820-84c2-50c996c14b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-39349" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f4f895a2-2d12-54fe-840a-ba0d65650236" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539048}}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538232}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069582} :activity/id #uuid "49e747aa-67fa-5a5d-956a-efff2f77cce1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363 :topic/id #uuid "98de9504-2d24-511e-8d7b-74400569101d" :topic/math-academy-id 477} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070005 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =1+4+9 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve. \n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." :tutorial/id #uuid "196ab182-655a-56ac-9e26-89a3d2835dde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 727 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a126e5fd-355d-55ad-b4aa-e14b49d64d66" :step/math-academy-id 652096 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068553 :question/id #uuid "f781ef9b-491f-5df4-a273-94f30ce79185" :question/math-academy-id "e-389"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535071}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "30e05a77-f052-5774-b6ca-d2d8db22d397" :step/math-academy-id 652098 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070006 :tutorial/content "If ![](images/9a/9ac10abedf6cecc066a4858ab2859e3c3038a43b10ac3261bd4120d0dc861990.svg) on ![](images/88/88e5bb9f44a0b3bb16a7e0e5461c5cc3f1f0bef907eda396d725caf367f29fb5.svg) the area ![](images/6b/6bd69882ede929379fa9babdf49386dd09138f26cabc6a4d617bbac5e92d21a5.svg) under the curve ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) over the interval ![](images/2f/2f2185d0962aceaafb8dbacf7486e61ccafbb77a54e961b33e886dd3b79a20df.svg) can be approximated using a left Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/85/8547bd96f866100db3ed28f3f23e3446ab5eff50632c425c23c2916c89f823db.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n![](images/c9/c92cf3627eeb4798f2f72577c3df4788c311b59b575ccbfa17caf14f39dacff1.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/4a/4a4a2bd99240362f13e0954449f58ab1c21ae7d29ffec2a1b2c8f1350f6fbec7.svg) and ![](images/09/0979b76b241c9ddb5f2e3d097811c00175e0f9242fa36cc4bff45e2963c3dd8f.svg) are the endpoints of the interval, ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is the number of rectangles, and the step size ![](images/bd/bd77198ba2d349a9ae25a0866060aa94624d6e255cabe04e818dc06e755ae85c.svg) is given by\n![](images/c9/c9f5c5a2741bae825236da3287d754a2526954e894011ace34f5cde5e65a4b45.svg)\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the ![](images/a7/a7e281a725fb2bff766e9ddb72c85bb6f7a8e7d45de6212372795ba1764d5ddc.svg) which gives the following general formula:\n\n![](images/27/277114008d8847a523fb417695fe2f62a4f7ff08efc0b9b8ea58556029ea9c5e.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2ae388a-a2b0-584b-bf06-7b66f82ce253" :tutorial/math-academy-id 729 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Left Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "fc16ac0f-77ae-504b-9997-be48eb6a1191" :step/math-academy-id 652097 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070007 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta x$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFirst, note that the domain of $f(x)$ has been *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n [1,4] &= [1, 2] \\cup [2, 3.5] \\cup [3.5, 4] .\n}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table. \n\n![](images/2e/2e8563480f46ff335042c3c52a6f60983f871dccb886c8cf3ca0844504a73db6.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our left Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the left endpoint of each interval. \n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n![](images/8f/8f62020b1888f7479665b04028acb822fdccfa9049a37e306ea30ce0aa703ac7.svg)\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}1} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}4} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}3.5-2})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-3.5}) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}1} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} +{\\color{red}4} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5}  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&=1+6+3\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= 10.\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86c2c7f1-e8cd-59f5-a5ed-1be84ec0a424" :tutorial/math-academy-id 730 :tutorial/title "Left Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "b3afc098-fc93-5ec7-9554-3989f6fd68d6" :step/math-academy-id 652108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068554 :question/id #uuid "6220f2e1-e654-5a30-a0f8-377bb0d64bc7" :question/math-academy-id "e-390"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535080}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "bd0626fb-e960-598c-8d94-fb531e7ccce6" :step/math-academy-id 652109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068640 :question/id #uuid "797ec111-8928-5875-8f94-9a423ff558f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7228"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535090}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "c489e48f-0e35-581c-85cc-0974d9100b36" :step/math-academy-id 652101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068641 :question/id #uuid "60eae905-fa87-5eac-bff2-8326ae41ca1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7230"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535060}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"} :step/id #uuid "c4ba3e71-564e-5615-b646-afbf5d8127c9" :step/math-academy-id 652105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069981 :tutorial/content "Whether the left Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/88/883b1d5faacf246557319e1f2b4fa253a06f657c589b24e5ec27eb35c00528c5.png)\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then  the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/ab/ab23c5b0d95a6bd78ac337266bb504c63b528d756548cbcd38e8b236ef140bc3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe559575-cc25-5a45-a1b2-fa3254bed0b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4372 :tutorial/title "Overestimates and Underestimates"} :step/id #uuid "02dd1ff6-87f8-57cd-b528-ab8c67dc87b7" :step/math-academy-id 652104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069721}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas With the Left Riemann Sum" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186069000}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125655} :activity/id #uuid "feaa62a5-6a44-51be-9efd-89d2c7e4938d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/id #uuid "a059bba1-760f-57e5-af3d-31e6c7347897" :topic/math-academy-id 1947} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125892 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](images/b4/b48de1dcd3a240a1f1904dd290ef0665fc603c4cc5f99915915c6a12f79d74d9.svg)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $e$ | $1/e$ | $e$ | $1/e$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b8b379fb-f7e4-5cdb-b25a-69a6c2ae9cf2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7879 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "85def1da-b072-51a0-ae08-591a0d352331" :step/math-academy-id 45426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124219 :question/id #uuid "14c9cf83-8dce-598b-b9cb-ec22966d8fac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048621}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484239}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"} :step/id #uuid "ac225680-0726-58b8-b03b-f62ad289bb51" :step/math-academy-id 45427 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124220 :question/id #uuid "84d1abb1-8b2b-5180-8924-825386924270" :question/math-academy-id "e-12588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446489}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"} :step/id #uuid "7c1f6a3a-a0f9-5d34-85e4-3cbe4e6bca0e" :step/math-academy-id 45430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125893 :tutorial/content "Consider a function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ and a closed and bounded region $D$ enclosed by several curves, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/04/042e4b736bdaa93033af34cc4ff738d1976a6595a448c8b697e717a74d19b67f.png)\n\nIn such cases, we need to search for extrema candidates among the following three categories:\n\nI. The critical points of $f$ that belong to the interior of $D.$\n\nII. The critical points of the function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}$ traverses each of the given parts of the boundary. In the example above, we would have to parametrize each of the $\\color{blue}4$ parts of the boundary separately.\n\nIII. The corner points of the boundary, i.e., the points where the different parts of the boundary meet. In the example above, we'd have to consider $\\color{red}4$ such points." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1ab24b0-a5c2-5d72-bc3e-614864010365" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7880 :tutorial/title "Determining the Candidates for Extrema on a Piecewise Smooth Boundary"} :step/id #uuid "8d1553dd-1fe4-5a34-9376-7547f1b2b500" :step/math-academy-id 45433 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124221 :question/id #uuid "138b3374-23fe-5705-87ed-e9e27a19e186" :question/math-academy-id "e-12589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049508}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446488}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"} :step/id #uuid "ccc80dce-cc5f-5576-95e2-9236c9281336" :step/math-academy-id 45434}] :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124737}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484306} :activity/id #uuid "014bb5c2-0eb1-526d-946d-25c9eb45386c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/id #uuid "a059bba1-760f-57e5-af3d-31e6c7347897" :topic/math-academy-id 1947} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead01c60-0b8e-5265-95fa-6b4cdaec742d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404352} {:answer/id #uuid "db0835b3-9555-5f10-b783-ce2279912fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404353} {:answer/id #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404354} {:answer/id #uuid "23d9111e-7ce1-561b-9f4f-29ebc2c46ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404355} {:answer/id #uuid "31a2fcdf-48af-528a-bc62-4f070d949ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c6b9bc-2cad-5aef-8fbc-0df5f811dfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32f5ebff-a024-5f63-b943-142f3a504252" :question/math-academy-id "q-86709" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/96/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "0072a223-d977-5b15-a887-a851fc97c6d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a98d925-ab88-52f8-884f-7e6b8120f7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483675} {:answer/id #uuid "3d039668-18b1-5d4a-9869-1e01eaef1bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483679} {:answer/id #uuid "589e8125-b8d8-5115-be4b-3627bde6a5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483729} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7a1cd-95f8-56b1-b089-7c0beaf8c25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484038} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c7bece-f4b8-5686-919a-a5cb331f67ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484057} :answer-field/id #uuid "750f6f9e-b6e0-5035-8e03-025f023dadec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50860c27-301d-5231-9268-fbee285d7c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-37146" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-3,0)$, and $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/d7/d7864a36585a145e009846b23142372caeff3a68f17c9d1f7d1cf17e642f9543.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1-1)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-4)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $16$ at the point $(-3,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "13d0691c-2114-546b-9bbe-2348cd351497" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e8f2b2-e881-54be-8bfe-5a90a63da983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321793} {:answer/id #uuid "58776b4d-dfad-5418-8461-511cc2520627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-5$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321843} {:answer/id #uuid "6a96826c-9c27-5fd9-82e2-7b681ef6be75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321978} {:answer/id #uuid "94d8e7cb-be9a-518f-a277-ee731c13382d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322333} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f68fc9-b908-5124-a8a9-322a05564d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321793} :answer-field/id #uuid "eedde66f-83fc-5633-be9a-580fbbeb170c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db6b4d4c-efe8-5d52-b8df-1c7481ef870b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37155" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(2,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+4 \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4 \\\\  & =13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4+0-8+4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0-12+4 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0+12+4 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the point $(-3,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "37a5ea02-37b7-5758-8571-8626bf94da23" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484340}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37223220-8ce2-5aaf-b13b-e51831642442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483667} {:answer/id #uuid "4141a628-787e-59cd-9b3b-4d9ed6c8303b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483690} {:answer/id #uuid "42c9840a-d25c-56c5-a13d-22ba7d99829d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483691} {:answer/id #uuid "91ce1e4d-9d5f-507c-b1a8-339a62262fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483870} {:answer/id #uuid "fde67504-6547-54d3-949e-2a67417d8d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483691} :answer-field/id #uuid "585700db-4a4c-5c97-98d7-30b257ff65a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "798ecbfd-5ca4-5e85-b538-375fc7b94df7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83400" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y$ on the closed triangular region  $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/30/30a739bfccac0a1ccc405b7ce1fd34afd46b5a2f436c852cef0bb38a13ec1e64.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,2),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =2(0)^{2}+(2)^{2}-4(0)-2(2) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)-2(0) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(0)-2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-4(1)-2(1) \\\\  & =2+1-4-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-3$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0),(0,2),$ and $(2,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "af6a13b7-97bf-5a56-b47d-072d6ba6485e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148c3c70-3db7-55f7-8111-88c0343f9bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[4]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351746} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b8adf-77e5-5a78-94b3-8706a2ac286c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351960} {:answer/id #uuid "9eacec04-ca0d-50a6-98e3-da77f9822ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352924} {:answer/id #uuid "cf33c688-22a9-5f87-ab62-f656a7ea1b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353362} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bb70c0-87ec-593e-850c-1115f0e6beea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351746} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd8154f-1898-5372-9fda-b77df68be7d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e5511a5-af5b-54bb-a9ab-113418cf34fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-86725" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy-x}$ on the closed triangular region  $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices and the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/42/42b9bcda74e29cf7012fd9576ee7bcea92bfc3f58fab4c43c854892afe3acc44.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2{e}^{(0)(0)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2{e}^{(2)(0)-(2)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2} \\\\ f(0,1) & =2{e}^{(0)(1)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,2) & =2{e}^{(0)(2)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =2{e}^{(1/2)(3/2)-(1/2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[4]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2{e}^{-2}$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2\\sqrt[4]{e}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "bcac2916-b3e2-509d-93c5-20d8fd6bdd8a"}] :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483555}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500417} :activity/id #uuid "1da851cc-95e3-5599-ae77-2e4ff094e963" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/id #uuid "a059bba1-760f-57e5-af3d-31e6c7347897" :topic/math-academy-id 1947} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500384 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dfe7cc3-7e1e-531b-9679-17d6282f66cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352805} {:answer/id #uuid "9480fe29-ef08-57e8-a109-77655e61781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352855} {:answer/id #uuid "959a80c9-8d6f-5ebf-854d-92e4451dd1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352863} {:answer/id #uuid "d4beecad-5b8a-50ab-b3aa-c19725a94642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353411} {:answer/id #uuid "e95fd2c0-5b91-5181-bf86-2c14f39b7349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352855} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1b2db2-672f-587c-8384-08bd0b27587d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6872646b-2fa0-5569-8460-8a72ff9bd7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-86723" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-1 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1+0-2-1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(2)-1 \\\\  & =4+0-4-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(-2)-1 \\\\  & =4+0+4-1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the point $(-2,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "00ed6b29-03a3-54e9-aaeb-46a22b4189a3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500415}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a620cea-c724-5cd1-81af-543f3d2c51dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\sqrt{3}$ and ${f}_{max}=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363543} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0d1a43-6f8b-5644-93e8-16948f17dafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=3-2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${f}_{max}=3+2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363544} {:answer/id #uuid "81a5b1aa-671f-52ad-9681-35461c2b2509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3+2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363545} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc0f0b2-6ebe-53e9-96ad-e380f02b0b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363546} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7e87b5-ff70-5a36-ac7c-77dc99ac2caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363545} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3adac3-45ef-501d-ba44-cfa5be5274bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48e27a98-5e9a-5abd-8c04-d1e75ddfe8ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-167162" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,-1)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,-1)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡t+3{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives two solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain three candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{3},0),\\,(0,-\\sqrt{3}),\\,(0,\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},0) & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}+2(0) \\\\  & =3+0+0 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(0,-\\sqrt{3}) & =(0)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,\\sqrt{3}) & ={0}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+2(-1) \\\\  & =0+1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3+2\\sqrt{3}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{3}).$"} :step/id #uuid "2e2150ac-f1ac-560d-bcf7-a241180b1570"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b85fe54-e88e-50e7-a167-e5f47c6caf9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348681} {:answer/id #uuid "ae644282-0673-52d6-b565-29bff873652c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349988} {:answer/id #uuid "decd099b-7990-5e6c-9d38-3fb5e4b3f69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350394} {:answer/id #uuid "e42eeb73-b5de-5a01-9ca0-dedfb055c39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350449} {:answer/id #uuid "f978cd4e-a6f5-5a68-8a84-167294111486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350394} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccbb50fc-642e-575f-bc5f-9e1b642c347e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a46a5d2-f50e-55ed-a8fd-136bbe52d472" :question/math-academy-id "q-83442" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives  \n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points: \n\n$$\n(1,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(-1,0),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,1) & =(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-1,0) & =(-1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,-1) & =(0)^{2}-(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and  $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and $(1,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "33ee7691-7413-57d9-a695-13ab29e0b850" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500384}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500415 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbbdac6-b001-5c5f-a60f-08cd76657100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282212} {:answer/id #uuid "53ba36d0-f558-562c-accf-8fca00ae7e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282403} {:answer/id #uuid "63258b6f-64b9-5b0d-a947-8f6914ff9da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282542} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9a08c9-cc69-53e4-a5f5-c418f62f71ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283499} {:answer/id #uuid "fa92ffe0-5aae-5f21-b93c-77bdca986153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283499} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2315c40-ba66-5f86-acdd-4cc57b9568cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ddfa127f-a279-50f9-9daa-00b98d8bc9d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-167160" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+2y-1$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1),$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/81/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & ={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =1-0+0-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & ={1}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1-1+2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =0-1+2-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & ={0}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =0-0+0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(1,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "7664bde3-a5cc-5860-8f11-c8f5b04ca956" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3b25a9-06c9-5f41-a968-8e309293d9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321603} {:answer/id #uuid "482d4f1f-144b-5a7b-9de9-9c2ad029db70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321716} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d6378f-0db9-5f1e-8409-7cce5146a925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322675} {:answer/id #uuid "cefc7b47-ab4c-51d4-a2bd-04794001d2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322763} {:answer/id #uuid "e387ed9c-203e-533b-b972-2b6c0b235b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321603} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb53a28-4118-5ba5-a904-3cb2c12eb512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14add615-dec7-5e1c-992f-2e9a1bbeb58c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37369" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}-{x}^{2}-2{e}^{y}$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/81/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1){e}^{0}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2-1-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1){e}^{1}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e-1-2e \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,1) & =2(0){e}^{1}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =0-0-2e \\\\  & =-2e \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0){e}^{y}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2e$ at the point $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $-1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "7e5430d8-004e-5647-bf66-5de0abfbdad8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500398}}] :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499641}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519620} :activity/id #uuid "79abe856-9095-50a4-97c9-7cfb29314a6e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/id #uuid "a059bba1-760f-57e5-af3d-31e6c7347897" :topic/math-academy-id 1947} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf37b4ad-f1a1-5a13-8dd8-d35dc56030e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404373} {:answer/id #uuid "5980b150-496c-55d6-8d60-52afa76e9b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404374} {:answer/id #uuid "d5651eb6-a478-51ea-b582-8eee402be230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404375} {:answer/id #uuid "e56bac34-6636-58da-9694-caed75b4198b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404376} {:answer/id #uuid "08b74c80-69bd-5d43-8f58-45d6f43cbc2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404375} :answer-field/id #uuid "6009c72a-9f51-5ad2-ae87-7dc2fbcb6754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "24be252f-8729-57e6-8082-158ca3588445" :question/math-academy-id "q-86724" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(3,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-2,0)$, and $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/75/752c84f5ab0fb122102629e9ceada21c1bba0b8f0e93b3fffe0639e4284badab.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}-6(-2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}-6(2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the point $(-2,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "0f8f73fe-f9ae-57fa-a170-226128cdae07"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233ac657-1b6c-5153-ae47-c73f6a18839b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404366} {:answer/id #uuid "1398af4c-7ac7-570a-a8f9-3e7b4fb50230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404367} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7069d6-ca3e-5408-8724-9f786c398052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404368} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9e5045-1ffc-5c1d-8753-b170299e5ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404369} {:answer/id #uuid "015fd256-8e43-5c2a-9c03-aa641f0627f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404366} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a29c76-a92d-5a15-9982-689804fc2dba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8392959c-a393-5152-9307-9f96b5859277" :question/math-academy-id "q-86722" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-4xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y,\\,-4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4y &=0 \\\\ -4x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-8$ | $10$ | $-8$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the points $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}).$"} :step/id #uuid "17b3d78a-4c11-56d5-8c1a-c9703c363db5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f08487e-af26-5c1f-962f-ed237732c2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=4$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446405} {:answer/id #uuid "0f5adf40-0426-5a10-8817-dc97f008412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-50$ and ${f}_{max}=20$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446406} {:answer/id #uuid "95dff366-2d95-5d09-afa0-223b43913ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-50$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446440} {:answer/id #uuid "e08526c7-85a2-54ab-b8bb-bc02be965b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-50$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446459} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d065ac-c348-5dd1-a57c-c5e5b0070028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-20$ and ${f}_{max}=20$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446459} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b2ba2b-f79b-5ccd-88fe-1d78549d2f08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc6c7d4b-ed06-5426-80f2-415a147181e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37149" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2+6x-3y+xy$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=8.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(3,-6),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,8),$ $(1,2),$ and $(2,8).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/06/06660df896e67cef560398ca24217cdba2e2b9a34fe40f1829b3e1d5d2db24e3.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,-6)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,8),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,8).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,8) & =2+6(-2)-3(8)+(-2)(8) \\\\  & =-50 \\\\ f(1,2) & =2+6(1)-3(2)+(1)(2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(2,8) & =2+6(2)-3(8)+(2)(8) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-50$ at the point $(-2,8),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $6$ at the point $(2,8).$"} :step/id #uuid "20a41044-e252-5c1f-903d-a7342279ea7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0461d323-c426-57d7-8a31-96c6ccb0b392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446403} {:answer/id #uuid "1b49ff3f-4e51-53f3-a015-1753de86c110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446409} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcbaf68-d293-51cc-a576-3b40121223bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446411} {:answer/id #uuid "49abf9f2-669a-576a-a19a-889756e18ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446419} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbe1154-dbaf-5151-a42d-da24649ac300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446411} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f37801-7fba-5407-99f3-56966d38068b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11bccf6f-4ced-5e74-80c9-222565fe2e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83418" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{3}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.$ \nYou are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region: \n\n![](images/21/21edc0f327ce949027b6cfb26789442ea0f7a3be7d7c9638d540dff77062870a.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0-1)^{3}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =(1-1)^{3}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(1,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "911eba01-d7be-5536-a2d5-ce849d22fea6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52624a61-59b3-504e-b08d-3b0fb6689de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446421} {:answer/id #uuid "57d35883-bcf4-5fb7-9e76-479830e77caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446422} {:answer/id #uuid "817543f2-9556-5cde-ba81-e834fd869803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446430} {:answer/id #uuid "a584c5a2-465a-5915-9a0f-ed12a25b03f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446445} {:answer/id #uuid "ca91afc3-4d89-510b-bc13-24a1f97adc1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446422} :answer-field/id #uuid "f768c06e-68ed-5c8a-96fe-b36cf58285fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2504620-1607-5645-b4d2-e55a27da4e3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37555" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{2}+y$ on the closed region \n$D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\}.$ \nYou are given  that $f$ has no critical points in the region and on the boundary of the region,  the points that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $(0,0)$, $(2,4),$ and  $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nNotice that we are told that $f$ has no critical points in the region.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0-2)^{2}+0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(2,4) & =(2-2)^{2}+(4) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(1,1) & =(1-2)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4).$"} :step/id #uuid "d08cd63e-5c2f-51e0-8828-4fef355f9f9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519650}}] :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518980}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525142} :activity/id #uuid "faee6521-d29c-5e69-8077-dd4f40c45a4d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/id #uuid "a059bba1-760f-57e5-af3d-31e6c7347897" :topic/math-academy-id 1947} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ee89eb-54ab-5070-99c6-c324c0f9f01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446425} {:answer/id #uuid "a52483f7-a6b6-5cc1-abb3-7db0b579ef56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446444} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ee3bb-67c1-5270-8d84-3e8e37bf67a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446447} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a76143-6f93-57f5-9713-8433062ded47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446448} {:answer/id #uuid "efcb8428-944f-5ad5-960c-f08b95cb6ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446425} :answer-field/id #uuid "81180c93-e1c2-5e9f-adf8-f0c58db4c467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" :question/math-academy-id "q-83437" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has no critical points." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "35938dae-f811-5e2e-ad95-963a9aa094b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180b8d8a-e5ba-53e7-85fe-0018dfc11e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}$ and ${f}_{max}=4{e}^{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446408} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8beee5-d16b-5ad8-81c1-9b0ed36361cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446414} {:answer/id #uuid "4d624490-8037-521f-bbf8-506c7e05ca1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2{e}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446420} {:answer/id #uuid "ece1eee0-81d5-54f5-aa04-0c45d7d5fd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\frac{1}{e}$ and ${f}_{max}=4{e}^{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446460} {:answer/id #uuid "efa9e65b-368c-592e-ae82-4a598d72fc36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}={e}^{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc7cdb8-c00a-5bcd-bca0-3c9f09586b74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8bdc5f30-6e9d-55a3-aacc-08dbc13064bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-167161" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(-1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-4,0)$, and $(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/dc/dc5a440d527fcf7583c4a341f08f2c6b5f6b4c1dc87b75286ea0a7f569d6babe.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(-1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-4,0),\\,(4,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4,0) & =-4{e}^{-4}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}} \\\\ f(4,0) & =4{e}^{4}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4} \\\\ f(-1,0) & =-{e}^{-1}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-\\frac{1}{e}$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4{e}^{4}$ at the point $(4,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "9f68dbce-6e45-599a-bfd1-c6b275925b2a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525148}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bc837a-bbcd-5027-bfb5-1c8984cd1027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446416} {:answer/id #uuid "601699b9-c391-5b8e-a88a-c98ed2ca1d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446423} {:answer/id #uuid "9f15347b-2581-5183-b776-03898fbfc72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446442} {:answer/id #uuid "a6701104-c6a3-5c18-9c13-31528c875706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446446} {:answer/id #uuid "cda725f3-ec0d-574a-ba5c-8fd6946ee6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446423} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7409f8-33ba-5d76-9294-d18b4e765a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "004a7cc4-06c2-5abf-9f5d-0dfb86998289" :question/math-academy-id "q-83434" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+2$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(1)+2 \\\\  & =2+0-4+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =2(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0+8+2 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0-8+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $18$ at the point $(-2,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "eab0fb9f-c4e9-5efc-b37f-9403a6a4f3b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525142 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c23c898-f1a2-5f4e-b367-26b24d6dcd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446413} {:answer/id #uuid "9e618bea-0b35-5168-96bf-90ae9dca8ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446441} {:answer/id #uuid "c070f661-5b43-5330-b698-b38f14c4686a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446450} {:answer/id #uuid "c137dca3-ade7-592f-92eb-70a9b9c0e301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446451} {:answer/id #uuid "d23eb270-d0e1-5d1c-a620-fc71aa88f9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446413} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2953ed-d903-58c0-9224-6075f8a7a3be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8052460c-148b-5893-b35a-1e1c771c0935" :question/math-academy-id "q-83423" :question/problem "Find the global minimum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe1aa21de9b6354e561eb1e82f6c83b25c71770470dc8501e6b491e35e2c391.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ is in the interior of $D$, we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(2,2),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =(1)(1)-1-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,0) & =(0)(0)-0-0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)(0)-2-0 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,2) & =(2)(2)-2-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,2) & =(0)(2)-0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0)$and $(0,2),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "edf9a3f1-f1e7-5cb6-855c-9ad5ddf8f9e0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cd4c18b-e511-5b14-9f13-a5b4cb4f47f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401581} {:answer/id #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401582} {:answer/id #uuid "77dac977-36a1-5c53-9361-88fca32b4abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401583} {:answer/id #uuid "d746e3e5-aa5e-5fa1-88ee-69128529ad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401584} {:answer/id #uuid "18156a3d-068d-5e69-8ef7-8771427933c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401582} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc59060d-a9ed-5533-b31b-311227554f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e7d00266-1ded-5f4c-819a-7467528496c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167165" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2x,\\,2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=0 \\\\ 2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$"} :step/id #uuid "fb52dde0-9ad8-55d6-b552-eb2fb08647fe"}] :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524376}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079315} :activity/id #uuid "67f9618d-2121-5f21-8ff1-2bbb8d89a5c7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048498 :topic/id #uuid "a4b9d7e7-30a6-550b-9a00-c1803847c09c" :topic/math-academy-id 613} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079718 :tutorial/content "The **second fundamental theorem of calculus** (abbreviated FTC II) states the following:\n\n> *If a function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ with $x\\in(a,b),$ and the accumulation function $F(x)$ is defined as*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> F(x) = \\int_a^x f(t)\\,\\textrm d t,\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *then the derivative of $F(x)$ is given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> F'(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_a^x  f(t) \\,\\textrm d t = f(x).\n> $$\n\nLoosely speaking, taking the derivative \"cancels out\" the integral and leaves us with the integrand. This happens because the derivative and the integral are *opposite* operations.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, let's define the accumulation function $F(x)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\int^{x}_{2} \\sin(t^2)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to differentiate this function to find $F'(x).$ Let's start by differentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ which gives \n\n$$\nF'(x) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\int^{x}_{2} \\sin(t^2)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the right-hand side is difficult. However, we can apply FTC II and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! This theorem saves us a ton of work and is especially useful when the integral is hard to calculate.\n\n**Note:** To gain further intuition for FTC II, it helps to work out an example in a simple case. Let's define the accumulation function $F(x)$ as an integral that we can actually compute:\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\int^{x}_{2} t^2 \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nComputing the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{2}^{x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{8}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{8}{3})={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches up with the original integrand. Taking the integral turned $t^2$ into $\\dfrac{x^3}{3}$ minus a constant, and taking the derivative \"reversed\" the process to give us just $x^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "74916d96-0a48-5708-ae4e-0cce1e62d532" :tutorial/math-academy-id 814 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1264f3b9-3675-53b2-b098-5abbdc342525" :step/math-academy-id 1562 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078312 :question/id #uuid "25a78c7c-e5c0-5048-b12c-6ed4848f5f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-438"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403829}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"} :step/id #uuid "7d22f17a-59e4-5bba-afd6-c10bb6724990" :step/math-academy-id 1563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078313 :question/id #uuid "b5e8576b-cd5c-5153-a492-1e8367456744" :question/math-academy-id "e-439"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393500}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "fab031c3-e2dc-5d4c-b664-d3474b78f564" :step/math-academy-id 1566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078440 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078314 :question/id #uuid "03bb67e2-fc45-591c-beaf-890929fa0e11" :question/math-academy-id "e-440"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329832}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "72b7cfa8-00b9-507c-9ab5-18f2aa69acf3" :step/math-academy-id 1568 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078441 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078315 :question/id #uuid "bda18914-8a35-5d61-9b6d-d2747035e80b" :question/math-academy-id "e-441"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345086}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"} :step/id #uuid "22b4af35-861c-5786-8dad-68e29d7aab28" :step/math-academy-id 1570}] :activity/title "The Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078643}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134813} :activity/id #uuid "77b214ca-9157-567d-b810-012cd4c3adca" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135104 :tutorial/content "Let's consider an inner product space $V$ equipped with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$ \nA set of vectors \n\n$$\n{U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}\n$$\n\nis called an **orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product** if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set\n\n![](images/69/69571997d87ed3f40ee7e9b0742075fbd5ee107892f6a9b4198f75f3f5a9fce3.svg)\n\nand the inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${V}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${V}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ &=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ &=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute each inner product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(2)+3(1)(2)+2(1)(-4)=2+6-8=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(-3)+3(1)(1)+2(1)(0)=-3+3+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(-3)+3(2)(1)+2(-4)(0)=-6+6+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${V}$ is an orthogonal set." :tutorial/id #uuid "f5f69210-8732-5a94-b16b-b7de520cfc66" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "520866ac-288a-5e25-aa54-cc9335b03e13" :step/math-academy-id 32389 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133686 :question/id #uuid "cd4ebf12-7e4c-5110-872e-c381ea9b2646" :question/math-academy-id "e-8712"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237781} {:db